.TH AS09 1 2026-11-12 "bcc09 0.16.2" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
as09 \- assembler for the Motorola 6809
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B as09
.RB [ \-guwk ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR object ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR binary ]
.RB [ \-l
.RI [ listing ]]
.RB [ \-m
.RI [ listing ]]
.RB [ \-s
.IR symbols ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR seg ]
.RI [ source ]
.br
.B as09 \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B as09
assembles 6809 assembly language into an object file for
.BR ld09 (1),
or straight into a binary image.
It is dev86's as86 for the 6809, and the assembler that
.BR bcc09 (1)
runs on the compiler's output.
With no
.IR source ,
or
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
A flag option is turned off by following it with
.BR \- :
.B \-w\-
shows the warnings that
.B \-w
hides.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " object"
Write an object file, for the linker.
.TP
.BI \-b " binary"
Write a binary image: the assembled bytes, at the addresses
.B ORG
gives them, with no symbols to link.
Without
.BR \-s ,
the image starts at address 0 (zeros up to the first byte); with it, at
the first byte, and the symbol file starts with the image's start and
end
.RB ( $start
and
.BR $end ).
.TP
.BR \-l " [\fIlisting\fR]"
Write a listing (to standard output if no file is named).
.TP
.BR \-m " [\fIlisting\fR]"
Write a listing with the lines that macros expand to.
.TP
.BI \-s " symbols"
Write the symbol table to a file.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
The module's name in the object file (and in error messages), in place
of the source file's.
.TP
.B \-g
Put only the global symbols (those exported or imported) in the object
file.
.TP
.B \-u
Take an undefined symbol as imported, as if
.B IMPORT
named it (rather than an error).
.TP
.B \-w
Don't print warnings.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the output files even if there are errors.
.TP
.BI \-t " seg"
Make
.B .TEXT
select segment
.IR seg +3,
rather than 0.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version (as in
.BR "as09 0.16.2" )
on standard output, and exit with status 0.
.SH "SOURCE FORMAT"
A line has a label (starting in the first column, with or without a
colon), an operation and its operands, separated by blanks or tabs, and
a comment after a
.BR ; .
A line starting with
.B *
or
.B ;
is a comment.
Upper and lower case are the same in operations and register names, but
not in symbols.
A symbol is letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B .\&
and, after the first character,
.BR $ ,
as OS-9's names have it
.RB ( I$WritLn ).
.PP
Numbers are decimal,
.RB $ hex
or
.BR 0x hex,
.BR % binary,
or octal with a leading 0;
.BI \(aq c
is the character
.IR c 's
code.
Expressions have
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.BR * ,
.BR / ,
.BR & ,
.BR | ,
.BR << ,
.BR >> ,
unary
.B \-
and
.BR ~ ,
and parentheses.
.B <
before an operand makes it direct (a page-0 address, through DP),
.B >
extended; an indexed operand is
.BI , reg\c
,
.IB offset , reg\c
,
.BR ,X+ ,
.B ,\-X
and so on, with
.B ,PC
or
.B ,PCR
for one relative to the program counter, and
.BI [ ... ]
for one indirect.
.PP
Besides the 6809's instructions,
.BI OS9 " code"
is an OS-9 system call, as OS-9's own assembler has it:
.B SWI2
and the call's code, a byte
.RB ( "OS9 I$WritLn" ,
with
.B I$WritLn
.B EQU
.BR $8C ).
.SS "Pseudo-operations"
.TP
.BR .TEXT ", " .DATA ", " .BSS ", " LOC " \fIn\fR"
Assemble into the code, the data, the BSS (which take no room in the
object file, and are cleared at start-up), or segment
.I n
(0 to 15; code is 0, data and BSS 3; see
.BR ld09 (1)
for where the linker puts each, and its
.BI __seg n DL
and
.BI __seg n DH
symbols for each one's start and end).
.TP
.BR FCB ", " .BYTE ", " DB " \fIvalue\fR,..."
Bytes.
.TP
.BR FDB ", " .WORD ", " DW " \fIvalue\fR,..."
Words, high byte first.
.TP
.BR FCC ", " .ASCII " \fI\(dqtext\(dq\fR"
The characters of a string (in single or double quotes).
.TP
.BI .ASCIZ " \(dqtext\(dq"
The same, then a 0.
.TP
.BR RMB ", " .BLKB ", " .SPACE " \fIn\fR"
Leave
.I n
bytes;
.BI .BLKW " n"
leaves
.I n
words.
.TP
.IB symbol " EQU " value
.TQ
.IB symbol " SET " value
Define a symbol (SET can define it again; EQU can't).
.TP
.BR EXPORT ", " PUBLIC ", " GLOBAL " \fIsymbol\fR,..."
Make symbols this module defines visible to others.
.TP
.BR IMPORT ", " EXTERN " \fIsymbol\fR,..."
Name symbols that other modules define.
.TP
.BI ENTRY " symbol"
Make
.I symbol
an entry: the linker loads the modules that the entry symbols need
(only those), and the first entry it reads is the program's entry
point.
.TP
.IB symbol " COMM " size
.TQ
.BI .COMM " symbol" , size
A common block, in the BSS: one, shared by every module that names it.
.TP
.BI ORG " address"
The address to assemble at (for
.BR \-b ).
.TP
.BI SETDP " page"
Tell the assembler what DP holds, for direct addressing.
.TP
.BR IF ", " ELSE ", " ELSEIF ", " ENDIF
Assemble lines or not, by an expression (IFC and ELSEIFC compare
strings).
.TP
.BI MACRO " name"
Define a macro, up to
.BR MEND ;
.I name
then assembles its lines.
.TP
.BR INCLUDE ", " GET " \fIfile\fR"
Assemble another file here.
.TP
.BR .ALIGN " \fIn\fR, " .EVEN
Align the location.
.TP
.BR FAIL ", " .WARN
An error, or a warning.
.TP
.B END
The end of the source.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if there were no errors, 1 otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR ld09 (1),
.BR ar09 (1),
.BR objdump09 (1)
